INT. VELODROME - TRACKSIDE INFIELD - EVENING

Steep banking of blond wood. Overhead lights hum. An empty stand rises in tiers. The track gleams like a polished spine.

MARISOL VENTURA-KHAN (20s, skinsuit, helmet strapped under her chin) sits on a folding stool, bare feet in cleated shoes, staring at nothing. AUGUSTO NKEMELU (50s, coach, tracksuit, stopwatch on a lanyard) crouches in front of her, adjusting her cleat.

She breathes. In. Out. A slow metronome.

He clicks the watch. Doesn't look at it.

A speaker crackles overhead.

                    ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
                (PA, echoing)
          Riders to the line for the individual pursuit, final.

She stands. He stands with her. She rolls her neck. He rubs her shoulder once, hard, the way you'd warm up an engine.

                    AUGUSTO
          Four minutes. Four-oh-seven. Same as practice.

                    MARISOL
          I know.

                    AUGUSTO
          You've done it a hundred times.

                    MARISOL
          I know.

                    AUGUSTO
          Then why is your hand shaking?

She looks at it. It is. She flexes it. Stops.

                    MARISOL
          It's the cold.

                    AUGUSTO
          It's seventy degrees.

                    MARISOL
          Then it's the lights.

He lets it go. Leads her to the bike. She mounts, clips in, and rolls up the banking to the starting gate, where a mechanic holds her saddle. Augusto stands at the rail, arms crossed.

                    ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
                (PA)
          Five seconds.

Her face is still. Her breath doesn't change. He stares at her, and for one second she looks up, directly at him, with an expression he cannot read.

                    ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
                (PA)
          Go.

She explodes off the line. The bike hums on the boards. She comes round the first bend, the second, her cadence steady, her back flat. He clicks the watch.

                    AUGUSTO
          (under his breath)
          Good. Good.

Lap three. Lap four. She holds the line. He relaxes, a small exhale.

Lap seven. Her cadence stutters. He leans over the rail.

                    AUGUSTO (CONT'D)
          Marisol!

She doesn't hear. Or she does, and ignores it. Her wheel drifts a hand's width up the banking.

                    AUGUSTO (CONT'D)
          Marisol, down! Hold your line!

She comes off the banking too early, wobbles, corrects. The stands are empty but he shouts as if they were full.

                    ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
                (PA)
          Lap eleven.

She is a half-second off pace. He sees it. He sees it again at the next split. The stopwatch trembles in his hand.

Then she does something unexpected. She sits up. Coasts. Rolls to a stop on the apron, unclips, and stands with her bike against her hip.

He vaults the rail, runs.

                    AUGUSTO (CONT'D)
          What are you doing?

                    MARISOL
          (calm)
          I'm done.

                    AUGUSTO
          You were on pace.

                    MARISOL
          I was on your pace.

He stares. She takes off her helmet. Her hair is plastered to her forehead. She hands him the helmet like a gift.

                    MARISOL (CONT'D)
          Tell them I withdrew.

                    AUGUSTO
          Why?

                    MARISOL
          Because I wanted to see if you'd run.

He looks down at his own feet. Then up, at her, and somewhere between rage and relief, his face crumples.

                    AUGUSTO
          That's a terrible reason.

                    MARISOL
          It's the only one I had.
